  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains dominant over the area this\nmorning, with the axis currently centered over Utah and southwest\nWyoming. Monsoon moisture continues to linger, however. Though\nsatellite derived PWs indicated a small decrease overall, these\nvalues are still in the 0.7 to 0.8 range. This should be more than\nenough moisture to allow scattered showers and thunderstorms to\ndevelop this afternoon. Given the lack of steering flow, these\nwill be primarily focused over the higher terrain, but would not\nbe surprised to see some drift into adjacent valleys. \n\nSubtle drying and warming will continue tomorrow, but guidance is\nno longer indicating the relative lull in precipitation previously\nanticipated, so am anticipating another round of afternoon and\nearly evening showers and storms like the next few days. Thursday\nwill also bring the hottest temperatures of the week, with many\nlocations running 5-7F above seasonal normals. Latest HeatRisk\nforecast indicates some pockets of red (major) HeatRisk values for\nsome western and southern Utah valleys, but guidance indicates\noverall lower temperatures and more relief overnight than from\nprevious heat waves, keeping the risk of heat related illness\nlower than it might otherwise be. \n\nBy Friday, the ridge axis is expected to return to the Four\nCorners before continuing eastward out of the area over the\nweekend. Another Pacific Northwest storm system is on track to\nmove over the ridge Friday into Saturday, pushing a frontal\nboundary into northern Utah. This will have a noticeable impact on\ntemperatures, with weekend highs averaging 5F below seasonal\nnormals. The front will also provide additional moisture and\ninstability to northern portions of the area, bringing more\nwidespread coverage of showers and thunderstorms. \n\nThe absence of the ridge will ultimately lead to a pattern change\nfor the upcoming week, with maxes staying on the cool side for\nthis time of year as a series of troughs impact the area. There\nremains significant spread in the ensembles, however, with regard\nto the timing, track, and strength of any particular feature, so\nexpected impacts remain unclear at this time.",
